E|471#0|1|2|0|1|fix verse_ref to 27:44|Gill "The thieves also,....one or other of them, not both" verified verbatim (biblestudytools.com Gill's Exposition Matthew 27:44) — work file mislabels verse_ref/full_note_ref as Matthew 27:43, the wrong verse (27:43 is "He trusted in God..."); on-tension, offers both Hebrew-idiom and sequential-repentance harmonizations matching Luke's convert.
G|0|1|0|0|0|Parity ok (1 reconcile excerpt vs 1 named-skeptic discrepancy entry). Ehrman real & genuinely presses this Mark/Luke clash (Jesus, Interrupted, HarperOne 2009, pp.64-69, section "An Opening Illustration: The Death of Jesus in Mark and Luke" — verified via archive.org fulltext: "Both of the robbers being crucified with him mock him" p.66 vs "explicitly not by both men...one of them mocks Jesus but the other rebukes" p.68), but the connective overclaims by folding in Matthew — Ehrman's cited pages compare only Mark and Luke, never invoking Matthew's account, so "Mark and Matthew show both criminals mocking" misattributes scope to him. Haley 1874 p.384 pd_work verified verbatim on archive.org (plural-of-class idiom at Matt.27:44/Luke23:39-41, Chrysostom/Jerome/Theophylact both-reviled-then-one-repented); defendinginerrancy.com link verified real, on-topic, on-allowlist (sources "When Critics Ask," Geisler/Howe).
